Additional Permissions

The licensor grants the following permissions in addition to the Elastic License 2.0 above.
Sentences marked "for clarity" state how the Limitations above apply to this software. All of
this section is part of these terms (including Termination and No Liability), and the
permissions are perpetual and irrevocable for every version of the software released under
this file, except as stated in Termination.

Definitions used below:
- "Association" means an apartment association, a housing cooperative, a condominium or a
  homeowners association (for example a korteriühistu, a taloyhtiö or a WEG).
- "Installation" means one running copy of the software with its own database.
- "Infrastructure" means servers, virtual machines, cloud accounts and domain names. Backups,
  monitoring, e-mail and messaging services used by an installation may be provided by others.
- A person, company or association "controls" infrastructure when it itself is the account
  holder or contracting party with the provider, holds owner-level administrator access, and
  can remove anyone else's access and keep running the installation without them.
- A "property management company" is a business that manages associations' buildings under a
  contract with them. Together with every organization that controls it, is controlled by it
  or is under common control with it ("control" as defined in the Elastic License 2.0 above),
  it counts as one property management company.

1. Own use. An association may run installations of the software for its own building or
   buildings and give access to its residents, owners, board members, staff and contractors.
   This is not providing the software to third parties as a hosted or managed service.

2. Help for an association. Anyone, for example a member, a resident, a friend or an IT
   contractor, may install, configure, update and maintain an installation for an
   association, paid or unpaid, on infrastructure the association controls or on
   infrastructure permitted by 3 or 4. Except as stated in the next sentence, this is not
   providing the software as a hosted or managed service. For clarity, installing, updating,
   running or giving technical support for installations of the software (only using an
   installation as described in 5, or helping its users use it, is not such work) for more
   than one association at the same time, on a continuing basis and for payment or other
   consideration (whether sold as a subscription, retainer or support plan, invoiced per job,
   or included in another paid service), is providing the software as a managed service under
   the Limitations above and needs a
   separate written license from the licensor, also when every association controls its own
   infrastructure; you are counted together with your company (as defined in the Elastic
   License 2.0 above). Occasional one-off jobs, such as a single installation or repair, are
   not on a continuing basis, even when you do such jobs for several associations. General IT
   services are not affected as far as they do not install, update, run or give technical
   support for installations of the software; including such work for the software in a
   general IT, administration or property management contract does not exempt that work from
   this rule. Hosting and maintenance under 3, and the remuneration described there, are not
   such a service.

3. A member, owner or resident hosting for their own association. A person who is a member of
   an association, or owns or occupies a flat, house or other unit in it, may host that
   association's installation on infrastructure they control. They may do so for each such
   association and for no other, provided they charge no fee for hosting or maintaining it
   beyond reimbursement of the actual infrastructure cost. Remuneration for serving as a
   board member or chairman that is not tied to hosting or maintaining the software is not
   such a fee. The first Limitation above does not apply to this hosting.

4. One association managed by a property management company. A property management company
   may host on its own infrastructure an installation for exactly one association that it
   manages, one at any time. The first Limitation above does not apply to this hosting.

5. Users are not hosts. People who only sign in to an association's installation, for example
   an accountant or an auditor who serves many associations, use the software on behalf of
   those associations; that is covered by permission 1.

6. What stays limited. For clarity, the following remain providing the software as a hosted or
   managed service under the Limitations above and need a separate written license from the
   licensor: (a) hosting installations for associations other than your own on
   infrastructure you control, paid or unpaid, except as permitted in 3 and 4; (b) a
   continuing service for the software, for payment or other consideration, for more than
   one association as described in 2; (c) offering the software as software-as-a-service or
   as a managed cloud. The licensor may grant such licenses, including regional partner
   licenses for markets the licensor does not serve, on request.
